---
title: v1 to v2
description: The shape of the jump from v1, and the order to make the changes in.
---

v2 changed how a pipeline is decided. In v1 the answer was assembled when your application
started; in v2 it is compiled into your assembly and the container only selects from it.

Almost everything below follows from that one change.

{% aside type="tip" %}
Two releases matter on the way. **v2.3** removed the runtime registry, and **v2.12** renamed the
diagnostics and tightened several contracts. If you are jumping straight from v1 to the current
preview, work through this page, then
[v2.2 to v2.3](/ergosfare.docs/preview/migration/v2.2-to-v2.3), then
[v2.11 to v2.12](/ergosfare.docs/preview/migration/v2.11-to-v2.12).
{% /aside %}

## 1. Reference the source generator

Nothing else works without it. It writes the registration, the composition table and the plans:

```xml
<PackageReference Include="Stella.Ergosfare.SourceGenerator" Version="…">
  <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
  <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
</PackageReference>
```

In every project that declares handlers, and in the composition root. The `Stella.Ergosfare`
facade already references it.

## 2. Replace assembly scanning with `RegisterGenerated()`

```csharp
// Before
services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .AddCommandModule(module => module.RegisterFromAssembly(typeof(Program).Assembly)));

// Now
services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .AddCommandModule(module => module.RegisterGenerated()));
```

Discovery covers your assembly and every referenced one that references Ergosfare.

## 3. Handlers take a context, not a token

```csharp
// Before
public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, CancellationToken cancellationToken)

// Now
public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
```

The token is `context.CancellationToken`. The context also carries per-dispatch state and the way
to stop a pipeline — see
[Execution context](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/execution-context).

{% aside type="danger" %}
Contexts are pooled and returned when the dispatch completes. **Do not keep one** after your
handler returns — no field, no closure captured by a background task. Copy the values you need.
{% /aside %}

## 4. Interceptors replace behaviours

Cross-cutting work attaches through four named stages rather than a wrapping delegate:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

A pre-interceptor returns the message the next stage sees, so returning the input unchanged is
the pass-through case. Post, exception and final cover the rest — see
[Interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/interceptors).

Order is `[Weight]` descending rather than registration order, so anything that relied on the
order you registered things in has to say so explicitly.

## 5. Ordering and selection are attributes

```csharp
[Weight(100)]                 // runs before lower weights in its stage
[Group("audit")]              // runs only when a dispatch names this group
[DiscoveryKey("reporting")]   // registered only when a selection names this key
public sealed class WriteAuditRow : ICommandFinalInterceptor { … }
```

`[Group]` is per dispatch; `[DiscoveryKey]` is per deployment. They answer different questions —
see [Discovery keys](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/discovery-keys).

## 6. Events do not need a marker

A subscriber is what makes a type an event, so a domain type in a project with no Ergosfare
reference can be published as-is:

```csharp
public sealed record OrderShipped(Guid Id, DateTimeOffset At);   // no interface

public sealed class NotifyCustomer : IEventHandler<OrderShipped> { … }
```

Existing `IEvent` messages keep working. This arrived in v2.12 — earlier v2 releases required
the marker.

## 7. Build, and read the diagnostics

This is where most of the migration effort actually lands, and it is worth the time. The build
now reports what the runtime used to discover:

| Code | Means |
|---|---|
| `ERGO005` | this dispatch can never reach a handler |
| `ERGO006` | only subtypes of the dispatched type are handled |
| `ERGO007` | no dispatch can reach this handler |
| `ERGO010` | two handlers claim one message at the same level |
| `ERGO018` | a `Register` call names its type at run time |

None of these is new behaviour. `ERGO005` describes a call that already threw; `ERGO007`
describes code that already never ran. Work through them rather than suppressing them — see
[Working with diagnostics](/ergosfare.docs/preview/best-practices/diagnostics).

## 8. Mark your composition root, if it is a library

```xml
<ErgosfareCompositionRoot>true</ErgosfareCompositionRoot>
```

Only needed when the project that sees the whole application is not an executable.

## What you get for it

Worth knowing what the work buys, since most of it is mechanical:

- **A dispatch stops deciding.** Handler selection and interceptor ordering are compiled, and
  most pipelines run as a straight line of calls.
- **Mistakes surface at build time.** A missing handler, a contested one, a handler nothing
  reaches — all of it before the process starts.
- **NativeAOT is a first-class case**, because nothing on the dispatch path needs a JIT to close
  a generic.

## Checklist

1. Add the source generator to handler projects and the composition root.
2. Swap assembly scanning for `RegisterGenerated()`.
3. Change handler signatures to take `ErgosfareContext`.
4. Rewrite behaviours as interceptors in the right stage.
5. Add `[Weight]` where order was implicit.
6. Build, and work through the diagnostics.
7. Continue with [v2.2 to v2.3](/ergosfare.docs/preview/migration/v2.2-to-v2.3) and
   [v2.11 to v2.12](/ergosfare.docs/preview/migration/v2.11-to-v2.12).
